    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.add_named_graph - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.add_named_graph.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_named_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add a well-known small graph by name.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_named_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add a well-known small graph by name.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.add_named_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Add a well-known small graph by name.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nAdd a well-known small graph by name.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L11444-L11614){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.add_named_graph(\n    name,\n    type=None,\n    label=True,\n    rel=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Named graph](../assets/graphs/add-named-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Three graphs from the named-graph registry side by side: the house, a square with a triangular roof; the Petersen graph, an outer five-cycle joined by spokes to an inner five-pointed star; and the octahedral graph, a hexagon in which every node is joined to all the others except the one opposite it.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nAdds one of the named graphs that come up again and again in graph theory and network\nanalysis: the Petersen graph, the Platonic solids, the small cages, and other standard\nexamples and counterexamples. Names are case-insensitive, and spaces or hyphens may\nstand in for underscores (`\"Krackhardt kite\"` works as well as `\"krackhardt_kite\"`).\n\n| Name | Nodes | Edges | Description |\n|------|------:|------:|-------------|\n| `bull` | 5 | 5 | A triangle with two pendant horns |\n| `butterfly` | 5 | 6 | Two triangles sharing one node (the bowtie) |\n| `chvatal` | 12 | 24 | Smallest triangle-free 4-regular graph needing 4 colors |\n| `cubical` | 8 | 12 | Skeleton of the cube, the 3-dimensional hypercube |\n| `desargues` | 20 | 30 | Cubic symmetric; Levi graph of the Desargues configuration |\n| `diamond` | 4 | 5 | Complete graph K4 with one edge removed |\n| `dodecahedral` | 20 | 30 | Skeleton of the dodecahedron |\n| `franklin` | 12 | 18 | Cubic bipartite graph that embeds on the Klein bottle |\n| `frucht` | 12 | 18 | Smallest cubic graph with no symmetry |\n| `grotzsch` | 11 | 20 | Smallest triangle-free graph needing 4 colors |\n| `heawood` | 14 | 21 | The (3,6)-cage, Levi graph of the Fano plane |\n| `hoffman_singleton` | 50 | 175 | The (7,5)-cage, a 7-regular Moore graph of diameter 2 |\n| `house` | 5 | 6 | A square with a triangular roof |\n| `house_x` | 5 | 8 | The house graph with both diagonals of the square |\n| `icosahedral` | 12 | 30 | Skeleton of the icosahedron |\n| `krackhardt_kite` | 10 | 18 | Network where degree, betweenness, closeness disagree |\n| `mcgee` | 24 | 36 | The (3,7)-cage, the smallest cubic graph of girth 7 |\n| `moebius_kantor` | 16 | 24 | Cubic symmetric; generalized Petersen graph GP(8,3) |\n| `nauru` | 24 | 36 | Cubic symmetric; generalized Petersen graph GP(12,5) |\n| `octahedral` | 6 | 12 | Skeleton of the octahedron, K(2,2,2) |\n| `pappus` | 18 | 27 | Cubic symmetric; Levi graph of the Pappus configuration |\n| `petersen` | 10 | 15 | The (3,5)-cage, a common counterexample in graph theory |\n| `sedgewick_maze` | 8 | 10 | Small maze from Sedgewick's Algorithms |\n| `shrikhande` | 16 | 48 | Strongly regular, like the 4 x 4 rook's graph but not it |\n| `tetrahedral` | 4 | 6 | Skeleton of the tetrahedron, the complete graph K4 |\n| `truncated_cube` | 24 | 36 | Skeleton of the truncated cube |\n| `truncated_tetrahedron` | 12 | 18 | Skeleton of the truncated tetrahedron |\n| `tutte` | 46 | 69 | Cubic, planar, 3-connected graph with no Hamiltonian cycle |\n| `tutte_coxeter` | 30 | 45 | The (3,8)-cage, Levi graph of Cremona-Richmond |\n\nWhere NetworkX has the same graph, the node numbering matches its numbering, with node\n`0` there being the first node created here (NetworkX labels the Shrikhande graph's nodes\n`(x, y)`, which is node `4x + y` here); the cubic Hamiltonian graphs follow their\nLCF notation (see `add_lcf_graph()`), so their first `n` edges in order form a\nHamiltonian cycle. In a directed graph every edge points from the earlier-created node\nto the later one. The graph is appended without touching or connecting to existing\nnodes, and the whole construction is recorded as a single step.\n\nNamed graphs are useful for:\n\n- checking algorithms against published values (the Petersen graph has chromatic number\n  `3`, girth `5`, and 120 automorphisms)\n- counterexamples: the Tutte graph refutes Tait's conjecture, and the Frucht graph is\n  cubic with no symmetry at all\n- small, recognizable examples for teaching and documentation\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Name of the graph, from the table above.\n\n<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),\n    no `type` attribute is set.\n\n<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; a label\n    that is already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, the\n    nodes are left unlabelled.\n\n<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),\n    no `rel` attribute is set.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `name=` is not one of the names above. The error message lists the valid names.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~add_kneser_graph`) \n\n:   Add a Kneser graph.\n\n[](`~add_generalized_petersen_graph`) \n\n:   Add a generalized Petersen graph.\n\n[](`~betweenness_centrality`) \n\n:   Compute the betweenness centrality of every node.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe Petersen graph has `10` nodes and `15` edges, every node has degree `3`, and its\nshortest cycle has length `5`:\n\n::: {#b8a299f0 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\npetersen = ec.Graph().add_named_graph(\"petersen\")\n\npetersen.count_nodes(), petersen.count_edges(), petersen.girth()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(10, 15, 5)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe Tutte graph is cubic, planar and 3-connected, yet it has no Hamiltonian cycle, which\ndisproved Tait's 1884 conjecture that every such graph has one:\n\n::: {#77ef5dda .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntutte = ec.Graph().add_named_graph(\"tutte\")\n\ntutte.is_k_regular(3), tutte.is_planar(), tutte.node_connectivity()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(True, True, 3)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### The most central node in Krackhardt's kite\n\nKrackhardt's kite is a small social network built to show that the most central node\ndepends on the measure. Node `\"4\"` has the most ties, but node `\"8\"`, the only link to the\nkite's tail, lies on the most shortest paths. The kite is drawn twice with the same\npositions, its nodes sized by degree on the left and by betweenness on the right:\n\n::: {#9209888e .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nkite = ec.Graph().add_named_graph(\"Krackhardt kite\").layout_kamada_kawai()\nscores = kite.betweenness_centrality(return_dict=True)\nkite.set_node_attrs(\"betweenness\", scores)\n\nnodes = kite.get_node_df()\ntop = nodes.sort(\"degree\", descending=True)[\"label\"][0]\nbroker = nodes.sort(\"betweenness\", descending=True)[\"label\"][0]\n\ntop, broker\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n('4', '8')\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#211ff334 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nkite.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", size_by=\"degree\", node_size=9, label_by=\"label\",\n    width=320, height=320, legend=False,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\" viewBox=\"11.3 17.4 297.1 297.1\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"11.3\" y=\"17.4\" width=\"297.1\" height=\"297.1\" fill=\"var(--ect-3239c383)\"/>\n<line x1=\"140.0\" y1=\"119.3\" x2=\"129.5\" y2=\"88.8\" stroke=\"var(--ect-e5dfd507)\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" stroke-opacity=\"0.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<line x1=\"140.0\" y1=\"119.3\" x2=\"129.5\" y2=\"88.8\" fill=\"